cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Ghost-Ark: bounded governance receipts and deterministic enforcement
  primitives for LLM and agentic applications
message: >-
  If you use this artifact, cite the software. Ghost-Ark is a research
  artifact, not a product. See the abstract for what it does and does not
  establish before citing it as evidence for a security property.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Bhave
    given-names: Pranav
repository-code: https://github.com/Cubits11/ghost-ark
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Ghost-Ark is an AWS-native reference implementation of bounded governance
  receipts and deterministic enforcement primitives for LLM and agentic AI
  applications. It provides deterministic receipt canonicalization, signature
  envelope validation, tenant-boundary enforcement, fail-closed policy
  evaluation, and a replayable verification path, together with an experiment
  suite that measures its own limits.

  Scope of what the artifact establishes: it verifies what was recorded,
  signed, policy-bounded, and replayable under Ghost-Ark verifier rules. It does
  not establish semantic safety, truthfulness of model output, regulatory
  compliance, alignment, production readiness, or deployment correctness. The
  repository enumerates its open weaknesses in docs/research/EXPERIMENTS.md and
  docs/artifact/CI_COVERAGE.md, including a measured cross-runtime portability
  negative result: no two of the three tested JSON pipelines induce the same
  equivalence relation over receipt inputs, so canonical receipt identity is not
  portable across runtimes without a mandated runtime or a stricter admission
  profile.

  The associated measurement framework for correlated guardrail failure
  (CC-Framework) is developed in a separate repository.
keywords:
  - provenance
  - canonicalization
  - audit receipts
  - AI governance
  - verifiable evidence
  - agentic systems
  - differential testing
  - mutation testing
